      "actualVisualReason": "The balance holds the same marked 1 g on each side, with carbohydrate 17 kJ and fat 37 kJ. A schematic cube and droplet are not drawn-to-scale masses; bars are qualitative, not ATP counters. Equal-mass reference and energy labels are legible at 360 and clear at 680.",
      "positiveScienceReason": "Equal 10 g food-energy values 170 and 370 kJ give 37/17 about 2.176, a mass-normalized comparison. Palmitic acid C16H32O2 plus 23 O2 to 16 CO2 plus 16 H2O balances 48 oxygen atoms on each side. Seven beta-oxidation NADH times 2.5, seven FADH2 times 1.5, eight acetyl-CoA times 10 minus activation cost 2 yield 106 net ATP. Mass-normalized ATP is 30/180 about 0.1667 versus 106/256 about 0.4141 mol ATP/g, ratio 2.484; this is explicitly distinct from the food-energy ratio 2.18. A stipulated glycogen store 100 g plus 300 g water gives 4.25 kJ/g wet store versus fat 37, ratio about 8.71. Fresh changed coupling gives 83 ATP in the stated model, rather than claiming the palmitate number describes every triglyceride, glycerol or unsaturation state. Higher storage density does not imply faster energy supply.",
